  "response": "The democratic peace has an unusually long intellectual pedigree, and that history shapes how its competing explanations are weighed. Immanuel Kant laid out an early philosophical argument for peace among republics in his essay \"Perpetual Peace.\" Kant published \"Perpetual Peace\" in 1815, whereas Michael Doyle revived Kantian arguments about liberal states in a 1983 article in Philosophy and Public Affairs, so Kant's argument came long before Doyle's revival of it. Doyle's intervention moved the Kantian vision from political philosophy into international relations, where it became a set of claims about how liberal states actually behave toward one another and could therefore be tested.\n\nThe empirical regularity at the center of the debate is now among the most frequently cited findings in the discipline. Democracies almost never wage war against one another; that is, wars between two democratic states are extremely rare. Disputes over the coding of borderline cases, such as the War of 1812 or conflicts involving fledgling or partial democracies, have refined the pattern without dissolving it. Because the regularity itself is largely accepted, the scholarly contest has shifted from whether the democratic peace exists to why it exists, and that question has proven far more difficult to settle.\n\nTwo families of explanation dominate the field. The normative explanation attributes the democratic peace to shared norms of peaceful conflict resolution. The institutional explanation, by contrast, attributes the democratic peace to constraints that democratic structures place on leaders. On the first view, democratic citizens and elites externalize the practices of compromise and negotiation they use at home and expect the same from fellow democracies. On the second, electoral accountability, legislative oversight, and the slow mobilization of public consent make war costly and cumbersome for democratic executives. The difficulty is that the two accounts converge on the same prediction, and the literature has accordingly treated them as compatible rather than exclusive: at least one of two restraints operates, perhaps both. The normative attribution leads to the claim that shared democratic norms restrain democracies from fighting one another, or else institutional constraints on leaders restrain democracies from fighting one another. A design capable of separating these mechanisms must therefore find circumstances in which their observable implications diverge.\n\nThe most influential attempt to do so came from two scholars working with dyadic data on militarized disputes. Zeev Maoz and Bruce Russett tested normative and structural models of the democratic peace against each other. Maoz and Russett published their comparative test of the two models in 1983, and that publication indicates the shape of their result. Although the institutional restraint has strong intuitive appeal, the outcome of their test is one of exactly two possibilities, which cannot both be true: either Maoz and Russett found the normative model to be more robust than the structural model, or they found the structural model to be at least as robust as the normative model. The tension is settled by their own summary: Maoz and Russett concluded that the normative model received stronger empirical support than the structural model. That conclusion confirms that the two models had genuinely been set against one another rather than merely estimated side by side, and it established the normative account as the leading explanation for much of the following decade.\n\nSubsequent work has not treated the matter as closed. Critics observed that measures of democratic norms, often proxied by regime stability or the absence of domestic political violence, are difficult to distinguish from measures of institutional maturity, so that the purported victory of one model may reflect operational choices more than causal reality. Others pointed to democracies' covert interventions against fellow democracies, which seem inconsistent with deeply internalized norms of restraint. The sharpest challenge went further still. Sebastian Rosato argued in the American Political Science Review that both causal logics of the democratic peace are flawed. His critique shifted the burden of adjudication: rather than asking which mechanism wins a head-to-head test, scholars must now show that either mechanism operates as theorized at all, which is a considerably more demanding standard than the correlational comparisons on which the original debate rested.",
